# Stakeholder needs derived from the Baseline A context and public operational guidance.
requirement NEED-001
title: Local awareness of storage conditions
statement:
  Clinic personnel need to recognize current refrigerator conditions, temperature excursions, and monitoring faults while they are at the storage site.
rationale:
  Local awareness supports timely programme action and must not depend on wide-area connectivity.
source: CDC-PINK-5:Monitoring Vaccine Temperatures + PILOT-CONTEXT:CTX-EXCURSION
end requirement

requirement NEED-002
title: Remote awareness of alarms and faults
statement:
  Authorized public-health programme staff need to learn of refrigerator alarm and monitoring-fault conditions without visiting the clinic.
rationale:
  Remote awareness supports oversight and escalation across geographically separated service points.
source: WHO-EM01.2:1 and 4.6.5
end requirement

requirement NEED-003
title: Monitoring through infrastructure interruptions
statement:
  Clinic personnel need monitoring and local alarming to continue during prolonged loss of site power and temporary loss of remote connectivity.
rationale:
  The selected deployment context includes intermittent electricity and communications.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.12 + PILOT-CONTEXT:CTX-POWER-LOSS
end requirement

requirement NEED-004
title: Investigable historical record
statement:
  Authorized users need retained time-correlated monitoring, alarm, and fault records sufficient to investigate excursions and equipment performance.
rationale:
  A current indication alone cannot support trend review, excursion investigation, or demonstration of prior conditions.
source: CDC-PINK-5:Monitoring Vaccine Temperatures
end requirement

requirement NEED-005
title: Controlled employer data access
statement:
  The employer needs to control who can access its raw and summarized monitoring data throughout the contracted service period.
rationale:
  Hosting the data outside the clinic must not transfer programme ownership or grant unrestricted access.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.19-4.2.20
end requirement

requirement NEED-006
title: Interoperability with compliant equipment
statement:
  The employer needs the monitoring product to interoperate with compliant refrigerator loggers and external programme data systems without proprietary reinterpretation of cold-chain records.
rationale:
  Interoperability permits equipment and data-service choices to remain separable.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.1.1 and 4.2.21
end requirement

requirement NEED-007
title: Maintainable field operation
statement:
  A trained service technician needs to diagnose monitoring faults and replace serviceable energy-storage components at the deployment site.
rationale:
  Field service reduces avoidable monitoring outages at remote clinics.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.13 and 4.9.1
end requirement

requirement NEED-008
title: Trustworthy event timing
statement:
  Authorized users need monitoring records from logger-relative time to be associated with trustworthy absolute time and displayed in the configured local time zone.
rationale:
  Investigation and coordination require events from different systems to share an understandable time basis.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.1-4.2.4
end requirement

requirement NEED-009
title: Distinguishable absence and failure
statement:
  Authorized users need to distinguish unavailable readings, communication interruptions, and device faults from measured in-range conditions.
rationale:
  Missing evidence must not be presented as evidence of acceptable storage conditions.
source: CDC-PINK-5:Monitoring Vaccine Temperatures
end requirement

requirement NEED-010
title: Preserve human authority over vaccine disposition
statement:
  Clinic personnel need monitoring information to support, but not automate, decisions about whether vaccines remain suitable for use after an excursion.
rationale:
  Product monitoring is only one input to the programme's documented excursion-response process.
source: CDC-PINK-5:Temperature Excursions + PILOT-CONTEXT:Explicit exclusions
end requirement
